WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.701
-You look fantastic, buddy.
You always do.

00:00:02.701 --> 00:00:04.000
-You kept the beard.
-I did keep it.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.667
I kept the beard.
-You love it

00:00:05.667 --> 00:00:07.834
because everybody's saying
you're so hot right now.

00:00:07.834 --> 00:00:09.501
[ Laughter ]
-Stop.

00:00:09.501 --> 00:00:11.534
No, that's not why I love it.
-And you don't want to lose it.

00:00:11.534 --> 00:00:15.300
The last time I saw Jimmy
was in front of a lemonade stand

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:16.667
with our kids...
-Yeah.

00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:19.467
-...and I was like, "Oh, okay.
So this is a new look."

00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:23.734
And then I hear that everybody's
saying how hot he looks.

00:00:23.734 --> 00:00:27.300
And I notice it's still on.
-No, I just kept it on.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.734
I figured --
I'm just gonna keep it on.

00:00:28.734 --> 00:00:31.934
I have nothing --
I figured -- I don't know why.

00:00:31.934 --> 00:00:34.000
-You've pruned it.
-No, I didn't prune.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.868
-Really?
You didn't topiary your face?

00:00:36.868 --> 00:00:39.067
-No.
This is a non-pruned beard.

00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:41.167
Look at this.
-That's au natur?

00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:43.300
-That's --
Yeah, that's how patchy it is.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:45.467
-No scissors
have gone to this beard.

00:00:45.467 --> 00:00:46.868
-Zero.
Where would I put scissors?

00:00:46.868 --> 00:00:50.200
-I don't know.
Just a little number two.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:51.701
-No, that's why it's almost
coming in

00:00:51.701 --> 00:00:53.000
like a goatee
a little bit weird.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.534
[ Laughter ]
But it's not.

00:00:54.534 --> 00:00:55.934
I didn't --
I didn't do any trimming.

00:00:55.934 --> 00:00:58.434
This is all legit.
-Am I right, though,

00:00:58.434 --> 00:01:01.968
that we think maybe --
I think this is a good look.

00:01:01.968 --> 00:01:03.801
Like, you're into it
and you're feeling it.

00:01:03.801 --> 00:01:06.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:09.033
-I don't know.
I think something has to change.

00:01:09.033 --> 00:01:10.734
I just think maybe --
You know what it was?

00:01:10.734 --> 00:01:13.634
I saw Dolly Parton.
I went to the Smoky Mountains.

00:01:13.634 --> 00:01:14.901
I did something with Dolly,
and I went --

00:01:14.901 --> 00:01:17.300
-Dollywood -- you know,
your wife and I got lost

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:20.501
after we were road-tripping
around America in an RV.

00:01:20.501 --> 00:01:22.901
I can't recommend it more.

00:01:22.901 --> 00:01:24.300
You get to do
whatever you want to do.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.567
You like a place, you stay.
You leave, hoo!

00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:27.868
[ Laughter ]

00:01:27.868 --> 00:01:31.167
And we went to Dollywood,
and we drove around for like

00:01:31.167 --> 00:01:33.400
five hours
in the Smoky Mountains.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:34.634
And you know
when you're road-tripping,

00:01:34.634 --> 00:01:37.200
you kind of --
you know, five hours,

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.701
you're ready to call it a night.

00:01:38.701 --> 00:01:40.734
And we pulled right back,
and we were like,

00:01:40.734 --> 00:01:43.200
"Let's just pull over
and sleep for the night."

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.834
We go to pull over,
and we go to

00:01:44.834 --> 00:01:47.400
right where we left
five hours ago.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:48.667
It was like...
[ Laughter ]

00:01:48.667 --> 00:01:52.834
It was like Winnie the Pooh's
loop or something.

00:01:52.834 --> 00:01:54.400
And I was like,
"The Bermuda Triangle

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.334
is in the Smoky Mountains!"
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.334 --> 00:01:58.033
-"We can't leave this place!"
Yeah.

00:01:58.033 --> 00:02:00.901
But how magical was that?
-Oh, but Dollywood was awesome.

00:02:00.901 --> 00:02:03.167
And Dolly Parton --
I mean, come on!

00:02:03.167 --> 00:02:04.968
Legend!
-So I felt like I should be

00:02:04.968 --> 00:02:07.968
a Smoky Mountain man, a mystery.

00:02:07.968 --> 00:02:10.167
And so it inspired me
to just grow a beard.

00:02:10.167 --> 00:02:11.667
-Did Dolly see the beard?

00:02:11.667 --> 00:02:14.200
-No.
She inspired me for the beard.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.534
-Okay.
-Do you think Dolly

00:02:15.534 --> 00:02:17.400
will enjoy the beard?
-Well, I think we need to know

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.400
because everything
Dolly likes is awesome.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.334
-Yeah, true, yeah!
[ Laughter ]

00:02:21.334 --> 00:02:22.801
I want to talk
to you about this.

00:02:22.801 --> 00:02:24.334
'Cause you had a crazy summer.
I didn't really

00:02:24.334 --> 00:02:26.501
get to catch up with you
when I saw you last week.

00:02:26.501 --> 00:02:28.901
But I want to talk about
a couple things.

00:02:28.901 --> 00:02:30.400
First of all,
you went to a wedding.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:31.734
Okay?
-Oh, yes.

00:02:31.734 --> 00:02:34.267
-Yes, you went to
Britney Spears' wedding.

00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:36.634
-Yep.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:36.634 --> 00:02:39.667
Look at this.
There's you, Madonna,

00:02:39.667 --> 00:02:43.567
Selena Gomez, Donatella Versace,
Paris Hilton.

00:02:43.567 --> 00:02:45.968
What -- How long
have you known Britney?

00:02:45.968 --> 00:02:48.434
-Some serious O.G.
ladies right there.

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:51.868
-That is a good
gang of girls there, yeah.

00:02:51.868 --> 00:02:56.033
-I -- you know,
I made friends with Britney

00:02:56.033 --> 00:02:57.667
about a year and a half ago.

00:02:57.667 --> 00:03:02.567
And we've sort of had
some secret exchanges.

00:03:02.567 --> 00:03:05.434
And I know
that sounds nefarious.

00:03:05.434 --> 00:03:08.934
-Yeah.
-It was exactly the opposite.

00:03:08.934 --> 00:03:12.300
It was very connective
and honest.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:15.434
And we've both been through
a lot of things.

00:03:15.434 --> 00:03:20.133
We have some parallels that are
unique in their circumstances,

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:23.434
but, also, I think the things
that we go through in life,

00:03:23.434 --> 00:03:28.000
although these circumstances
may seem so next-level,

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.868
the feelings of growing up,
struggling,

00:03:30.868 --> 00:03:35.133
all the stuff
that we've both gone through,

00:03:35.133 --> 00:03:37.767
is something that I felt
connected to her.

00:03:37.767 --> 00:03:40.000
And I'm not a real
reach-out kind of person.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.567
You know, I'm not Hollywood Bob.
I'm not slick.

00:03:42.567 --> 00:03:46.501
I just -- the humanity
in what she was going through,

00:03:46.501 --> 00:03:49.367
trying to get her freedom back,

00:03:49.367 --> 00:03:51.868
was something
I felt compelled to reach out.

00:03:51.868 --> 00:03:54.334
And we found
a deep connection there.

00:03:54.334 --> 00:03:56.234
-Yeah. Good for you.
I'm glad you reached out.

00:03:56.234 --> 00:03:58.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:58.434 --> 00:04:00.634
-And --
-It's tricky, you know?

00:04:00.634 --> 00:04:02.968
It's insane.
When you see everyone's life,

00:04:02.968 --> 00:04:05.000
you go, "Oh, wow.
They must have a --

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.534
They must have a great life."
No one really knows anything

00:04:07.534 --> 00:04:10.567
unless you --
and she was crying for help.

00:04:10.567 --> 00:04:11.934
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:04:11.934 --> 00:04:14.501
There is a human being
at the center of this.

00:04:14.501 --> 00:04:17.734
And so, we made friends --
like, genuine friends.

00:04:17.734 --> 00:04:19.467
-And did you have fun?
-And then I got invited

00:04:19.467 --> 00:04:22.234
to her wedding, which could not
have been more intimate.

00:04:22.234 --> 00:04:23.801
The people you see in that photo

00:04:23.801 --> 00:04:25.234
are some of
the only people there.

00:04:25.234 --> 00:04:26.934
There's only a few more.

00:04:26.934 --> 00:04:29.400
At the actual event,
it was intimate.

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.868
It was fairy-tale-ish.
It was very sweet.

00:04:31.868 --> 00:04:34.033
It was --
There was no press.

00:04:34.033 --> 00:04:37.000
It was the way
she wanted to do it.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:38.934
-Yeah, good.
-Which was really --

00:04:38.934 --> 00:04:43.167
also led to that photograph,
which was also a moment.

00:04:43.167 --> 00:04:46.467
None of us planned for it.
It just happened organically.

00:04:46.467 --> 00:04:48.167
-When was this --
When was this dance party?

00:04:48.167 --> 00:04:49.467
-Oh, then, that was later.

00:04:49.467 --> 00:04:52.567
I also thanked her for not
having a sit-down dinner.

00:04:52.567 --> 00:04:54.467
God, do I hate sit-down dinners!

00:04:54.467 --> 00:04:56.300
[ Laughter ]
-What are you talking about?

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:58.167
-Guess what --
we all got dressed up.

00:04:58.167 --> 00:05:00.701
We all wanna dance,
and we wanna hang out.

00:05:00.701 --> 00:05:01.868
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:01.868 --> 00:05:05.734
-No, you sit down, you eat.
No, no.

00:05:05.734 --> 00:05:07.067
-What?!
-No, you're eating.

00:05:07.067 --> 00:05:09.767
-Now I have to sit down.
And they always separate you,

00:05:09.767 --> 00:05:12.300
like, "We're gonna force you
into a new social endeavor."

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:13.601
[ Laughter ]

00:05:13.601 --> 00:05:15.901
The person you came with
that you want to hang out with

00:05:15.901 --> 00:05:17.767
is over there.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:17.767 --> 00:05:19.601
Why did you do that?!

00:05:19.601 --> 00:05:22.300
And now let's eat
some bizarre food...

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:24.067
-Yeah.
-...that you didn't choose,

00:05:24.067 --> 00:05:27.067
and -- and -- and I hate it!
-Sorry, sorry.

00:05:27.067 --> 00:05:29.501
So, she got --
They did the ceremony.

00:05:29.501 --> 00:05:31.667
She got
right to the dance party.

00:05:31.667 --> 00:05:33.968
We had the best time,
and I was like, "Thank you!"

00:05:33.968 --> 00:05:35.968
-Yeah, Britney!
We love you!

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:38.767
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:38.767 --> 00:05:41.701
I do love Britney.
-I hate sit-down dinners.

00:05:41.701 --> 00:05:42.968
-And then --
We won't ever do one.

00:05:42.968 --> 00:05:44.267
-Unless it's a dinner party.

00:05:44.267 --> 00:05:45.968
Then that's
a different situation.

00:05:45.968 --> 00:05:47.267
-We've had sit-down dinners
before.

00:05:47.267 --> 00:05:49.467
No, really lunches.
-But you know how weird I am

00:05:49.467 --> 00:05:51.601
about the places.
Like, we like to go to this

00:05:51.601 --> 00:05:54.701
one place 'cause you can
walk around in it.

00:05:54.701 --> 00:05:58.133
I don't like that formal,
stuck to the chair

00:05:58.133 --> 00:06:01.200
like I'm in the 1800s.
I don't -- I just --

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:06.133
When I go out, also, I finally
feel like I've got a feather.

00:06:06.133 --> 00:06:08.601
Like, I've left the house.

00:06:08.601 --> 00:06:10.634
The last thing I want to do
is sit and eat.

00:06:10.634 --> 00:06:12.501
I feel social.
Let's party!

00:06:12.501 --> 00:06:14.100
-No!

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:15.400
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:16.434
You know how to party.

00:06:16.434 --> 00:06:19.067
By the way, look at you,
super model,

00:06:19.067 --> 00:06:21.400
at the Dolce &amp; Gabbana
fashion -- in Italy.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:22.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:22.501 --> 00:06:24.667
Are you kidding me, Drew?

00:06:24.667 --> 00:06:26.300
Drew Barrymore,
you're unbelievable.

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:28.767
By the way --
-I feel so awake now.

00:06:28.767 --> 00:06:31.634
I mean, I wear only sweatpants

00:06:31.634 --> 00:06:33.968
with hole-riddled
vintage T-shirts.

00:06:33.968 --> 00:06:37.834
Like, I don't put a lot of
effort into myself in real life.

00:06:37.834 --> 00:06:40.601
I don't.
I'm very shlubby.

00:06:40.601 --> 00:06:44.634
And so it's nice,
after the pandemic and two kids

00:06:44.634 --> 00:06:49.234
and a real body change, like,
I just really lost myself

00:06:49.234 --> 00:06:51.300
for a lot of years,
and it's really nice

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:54.167
to try and put back in,
put a little effort in

00:06:54.167 --> 00:06:56.734
every once in a while.
-Yeah!

00:06:56.734 --> 00:06:59.467
You always --
I didn't know you --

00:06:59.467 --> 00:07:01.100
I know, but when --
Come on.

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:03.133
This is just --
I dress like Jeff Spicoli

00:07:03.133 --> 00:07:04.968
from "Fast Times
at Ridgemont High."

00:07:04.968 --> 00:07:07.434
[ Laughter ]
That is my daily attire.

00:07:07.434 --> 00:07:09.367
-Yeah.
-So, if you're, like,

00:07:09.367 --> 00:07:11.701
at work, at the park.
-And going for it.

00:07:11.701 --> 00:07:13.601
-Yeah, just put
a little energy into it.

00:07:13.601 --> 00:07:16.868
-Did you get to celebrate the
40th anniversary of "ET" at all?

00:07:16.868 --> 00:07:18.033
'Cause I know it was a big deal
this year.

00:07:18.033 --> 00:07:20.400
-It was such a big deal.
And my daughters and I

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:23.400
were planning
to go with Steven, and it was --

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:26.000
Spielberg, not to name-drop.
I feel so bougie.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:27.267
That wasn't the point.
-I think you're allo--

00:07:27.267 --> 00:07:28.434
Of all people,
I think you're allowed to --

00:07:28.434 --> 00:07:30.834
-"ET," Steven.
-Yeah, you're allowed.

00:07:30.834 --> 00:07:32.834
-The wonderful Steven Spielberg.

00:07:32.834 --> 00:07:35.167
And we're all so close,
and he knows my kids,

00:07:35.167 --> 00:07:37.000
so he was like,
"Let's make sure the kids go."

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:39.467
And I was like, "I'm gonna watch
'ET' with you and the kids,"

00:07:39.467 --> 00:07:41.834
and Grauman's Chinese
Movie Theatre

00:07:41.834 --> 00:07:44.234
where my grandfather's,
their great-grandfather's face

00:07:44.234 --> 00:07:46.467
is in the cement.
And, you know, I was...

00:07:46.467 --> 00:07:49.133
♪ It's the circle of life ♪
-Yeah, yeah.

00:07:49.133 --> 00:07:52.033
-And we have
planned it for months.

00:07:52.033 --> 00:07:53.934
We're so excited.
It's probably one of

00:07:53.934 --> 00:07:55.868
the biggest,
most important things

00:07:55.868 --> 00:07:57.801
that we've ever experienced.

00:07:57.801 --> 00:08:01.767
Only the day before, they both
test positive for COVID.

00:08:01.767 --> 00:08:04.267
[ Audience "Awws" ]
And I have to call Steven.

00:08:04.267 --> 00:08:07.868
I'm shell-shocked,
and we didn't get to go.

00:08:07.868 --> 00:08:10.667
So, we're doing a make-up
with hot-fudge sundaes

00:08:10.667 --> 00:08:13.400
and in his screening room,
which is awesome.

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:15.701
-Have they seen "ET" yet?
-Yes. Yes.

00:08:15.701 --> 00:08:18.968
They -- They --
They love it.

00:08:18.968 --> 00:08:20.868
But it wasn't like watching it

00:08:20.868 --> 00:08:22.968
in a movie theater
with Steven Spielberg.

00:08:22.968 --> 00:08:24.467
-Yeah.
-And we were opening

00:08:24.467 --> 00:08:26.567
the Turner Classic
Film Festival.

00:08:26.567 --> 00:08:28.467
We were gonna sit and do
a sit-down interview

00:08:28.467 --> 00:08:30.300
on the stage
with Ben Mankiewicz.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:33.000
It was this thing
I've looked forward to so much.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:34.834
So, everything happens
for a reason.

00:08:34.834 --> 00:08:36.267
-Frankie's probably
around your age, right?

00:08:36.267 --> 00:08:38.901
-A really exciting story
with no climax.

00:08:38.901 --> 00:08:41.367
[ Laughter ]

00:08:41.367 --> 00:08:43.767
-Not "ET."
"ET" was a great climax.

00:08:43.767 --> 00:08:45.534
[ Laughter ]

00:08:45.534 --> 00:08:47.901
I'm not gonna spoil it.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:47.901 --> 00:08:51.267
But Frankie is 7?
That's how old you were.

00:08:51.267 --> 00:08:52.434
-She's 8.
8 1/2.

00:08:52.434 --> 00:08:55.033
I was 6 when I filmed it,
7 when it came out.

00:08:55.033 --> 00:08:56.367
-Oh, really?
-I've been doing this

00:08:56.367 --> 00:08:59.634
such a long time.
-Wow! You've been doing it.

00:08:59.634 --> 00:09:01.968
You've been doing it
such a long time.

00:09:01.968 --> 00:09:04.167
-You know what's so funny, too,
is like,

00:09:04.167 --> 00:09:07.767
people literally are like,
"Oh, so 'ET' was the first?"

00:09:07.767 --> 00:09:09.701
I'm like, "Oh, no.
Oh, no, no.

00:09:09.701 --> 00:09:13.534
I started at 11-months-old,
Puppy Chow.

00:09:13.534 --> 00:09:15.567
[ Raspy voice ] And then I went
on to do a whole lot more.

00:09:15.567 --> 00:09:17.934
I did TV movies and commercials.

00:09:17.934 --> 00:09:19.767
-[ Raspy voice ] Let me tell ya
what the business was like.

00:09:19.767 --> 00:09:21.167
-I was in a feature film.

00:09:21.167 --> 00:09:23.734
By the time I did "ET,"
it was old hat.

00:09:23.734 --> 00:09:26.400
[ Laughter ]
You know what I'm saying?

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:28.701
So, that was not my first gig.

00:09:28.701 --> 00:09:29.968
-[ Normal voice ] Yeah,
not your first rodeo there.

00:09:29.968 --> 00:09:31.701
-I was a vet by then.
-Yeah.

00:09:31.701 --> 00:09:33.934
Can we talk about the show,
"The Drew Barrymore Show"?

00:09:33.934 --> 00:09:35.868
Today, by the way, you're back.
I missed you.

00:09:35.868 --> 00:09:37.267
I wanted to --
I love your show so much.

00:09:37.267 --> 00:09:38.400
-[ Normal voice ]
I love you so much.

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:39.934
-And I love you so --
You looked great.

00:09:39.934 --> 00:09:43.567
It was so good,
and it was so interesting.

00:09:43.567 --> 00:09:45.434
I love that you just
put yourself out there.

00:09:45.434 --> 00:09:48.634
You had your ex-boyfriend,
Justin --

00:09:48.634 --> 00:09:52.267
-Yes, Justin Long,
who I love so dearly.

00:09:52.267 --> 00:09:54.234
And, you know, I --

00:09:54.234 --> 00:09:57.033
When we first launched,
it's a big measuring contest.

00:09:57.033 --> 00:09:58.467
[ Raspy voice ]
Who's your first guest?

00:09:58.467 --> 00:09:59.534
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:09:59.534 --> 00:10:01.400
Show me the big guns,
the big names.

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:02.801
-Yeah, who do you got?
-[ Normal voice ] And I was

00:10:02.801 --> 00:10:05.300
like, "I want to ask
Cameron Diaz and Lucy Liu,

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:08.834
my dearest girlfriends who I did
'Charlie's Angels' with.

00:10:08.834 --> 00:10:11.801
We've experienced
everything in life you can.

00:10:11.801 --> 00:10:15.601
Travel, birth, life, divorce,
marriage, kids, you know --

00:10:15.601 --> 00:10:17.167
-Everything.
-Everything!

00:10:17.167 --> 00:10:20.133
And I'm so proud
of our endeavor.

00:10:20.133 --> 00:10:24.868
And I think I'm trying to make
this show as personal as I can

00:10:24.868 --> 00:10:28.667
as well as have it be
a daytime talk show.

00:10:28.667 --> 00:10:32.667
And I think there's such a rich
conversation about our exes.

00:10:32.667 --> 00:10:34.534
You know, where does
that friendship go?

00:10:34.534 --> 00:10:36.567
Listen, if they're toxic,
get them out of your life.

00:10:36.567 --> 00:10:39.701
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-But nothing to talk about,

00:10:39.701 --> 00:10:41.033
have a boundary.

00:10:41.033 --> 00:10:45.300
But if there was good,
rich history there,

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:47.467
how do we champion those people?

00:10:47.467 --> 00:10:50.901
How do we have them in our lives
in an appropriate way?

00:10:50.901 --> 00:10:53.767
He has this awesome, wonderful,
incredible partner,

00:10:53.767 --> 00:10:56.567
Kate Bosworth -- by the way,
what an incredible woman

00:10:56.567 --> 00:10:58.367
who would allow this to happen.

00:10:58.367 --> 00:11:00.634
If she's not my hero,
I don't know who is.

00:11:00.634 --> 00:11:01.968
-Yeah, she just said,
"Yeah, go for it.

00:11:01.968 --> 00:11:03.400
Go and talk
and have the conversation."

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:06.334
-How do we...
-It was so good.

00:11:06.334 --> 00:11:09.033
-...deal with our real lives?

00:11:09.033 --> 00:11:11.033
I've been doing movies
and playing characters

00:11:11.033 --> 00:11:12.467
and being
a part of storytelling,

00:11:12.467 --> 00:11:14.767
which is so
incredibly universal.

00:11:14.767 --> 00:11:16.934
Talk about "ET."
If that didn't open me up

00:11:16.934 --> 00:11:18.701
to the kind of life
I want to have...

00:11:18.701 --> 00:11:21.968
But ever since I had kids,
I've been wanting to know, like,

00:11:21.968 --> 00:11:26.267
how do you live a life that
isn't just a movie, that's real?

00:11:26.267 --> 00:11:31.100
And this has been
the best avenue and exploration

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:33.501
for me right now
to do this show.

00:11:33.501 --> 00:11:36.801
-It was emotional to watch.
But you guys were -- you came --

00:11:36.801 --> 00:11:38.400
You were at our wedding,
both of you.

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:40.834
-Yeah, by the way,
he was my boyfriend.

00:11:40.834 --> 00:11:43.200
We were at the wedding.
That was my date.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:47.567
I never cried so hard in my life
than Nan and Jimmy's wedding.

00:11:47.567 --> 00:11:49.968
Like, it got really awkward.
-No.

00:11:49.968 --> 00:11:51.701
-Like, it was too much.
You know?

00:11:51.701 --> 00:11:53.200
-And we had a tiny,
small wedding.

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:54.534
-Very small.
But Justin and I were there.

00:11:54.534 --> 00:11:55.667
-This is the picture
from our wedding.

00:11:55.667 --> 00:11:57.200
This is...
[ Laughter ]

00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:02.200
...Donatella, Madonna...
Everyone.

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:05.033
And Justin. Not bad.
-See, in storytelling,

00:12:05.033 --> 00:12:08.400
you call that a payoff.
-Hey, the callback, yeah.

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:10.601
But, yes, I mean --
-That was emotional.

00:12:10.601 --> 00:12:11.901
I loved seeing you guys together

00:12:11.901 --> 00:12:13.300
because I know
how close you were and fun.

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:18.501
-Also, you can say, you know,
we have changed.

00:12:18.501 --> 00:12:20.901
Here's where we are at
in our lives now.

00:12:20.901 --> 00:12:22.801
Here's what you're up to.
I want to celebrate it.

00:12:22.801 --> 00:12:24.868
He's in the best movie,
called "Barbarian."

00:12:24.868 --> 00:12:26.033
It's gonna be a classic.

00:12:26.033 --> 00:12:27.367
Does anyone here
like scary movies?

00:12:27.367 --> 00:12:28.834
-Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:12:28.834 --> 00:12:31.067
I think it's the number-one
movie in the country right now.

00:12:31.067 --> 00:12:32.634
-It is?
-Yeah. Officially, yeah.

00:12:32.634 --> 00:12:35.167
-Oh, my God!
I'm so happy!

00:12:35.167 --> 00:12:37.667
I had to watch it by myself!
It was a nightmare!

00:12:37.667 --> 00:12:40.234
[ Laughter ]
-Is it scary?

00:12:40.234 --> 00:12:42.601
-It's the scariest movie!

00:12:42.601 --> 00:12:44.234
-Yeah.
-It's so good.

00:12:44.234 --> 00:12:46.767
It just taps into
every layer of fear.

00:12:46.767 --> 00:12:48.334
-All right, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:12:48.334 --> 00:12:50.334
-It's really destined
to become a classic.

00:12:50.334 --> 00:12:52.200
How wonderful for him.
-Yeah, he's on tomorrow.

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:54.200
I will talk to him about this.
-Please do.

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:58.200
-Yeah, exactly.
Congrats again on the season.

00:12:58.200 --> 00:12:59.734
Every morning,
"Drew Barrymore Show."

00:12:59.734 --> 00:13:02.267
You better watch --
TiVo, set your DVRs.

00:13:02.267 --> 00:13:05.167
-TiVo!
-Drew Barrymore, everybody.

00:13:05.167 --> 00:13:07.601
Season 3 of
"Drew Barrymore Show"

00:13:07.601 --> 00:13:09.000
premiered earlier today.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:11.000
Drew and I are playing a fun
new game when we come back.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:12.634
Stick around.
It's a good one.

